Lots of them didn't make that march. And we wind up that night. And we stayed overnight. We stayed overnight. I think one night we stayed in this particular little place, where they took us in for a rest from that long march. We wind up in Gleiwitz, Gleiwitz, Germany.

INT: And the next morning--

SR: That's Oberschlesien. Huh?

INT: And the next morning?

SR: [SIGHS] The next morning, we got up. We got up, yeah. And we slept down there with a-- lots of dead. People were frozen there outside. And they took us on trains, separated us again, people here, people there. And they had trains waiting for us, again, those cow trains or animal trains.

And we started to travel. Didn't know where we're going. And and I wind up being sent-- this was in January-- being sent to Nordhausen, Dora-- Dora, D-O-R-A.

This was in January, and this was in February-- two months. So by the end of February, I think, we were in Dora, Nordhausen-Dora. And another camp, they go through the same thing, take away the clothing from us, gave us new prisoner stripe suits. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And they sent us to work in-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and I spent there till they put us in-- in a place underneath the ground. And I think we used to-- to make the-- those weapons they sent with no people, VF1 or F2 or whatever.

INT: Yeah.

SR: Yeah. I used to put the wings together, you know, at night, because mine job was a night shift job. Yeah, doing, because it never stopped.

INT: When you said on the march, from Buna, you said-- said many people died, didn't get up in the morning, just died, frozen there. Did you bury the dead?

SR: We didn't. They took them away.

INT: They just took them away?

SR: Yeah. I, myself, counted like 18 dead-- never got up, because they didn't have-- they didn't have room or they didn't care where to put us in, in inside, to keep a little warm, you know? [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

How did I survive? OK. How did the other ones survive? By luck. I-- you know, you-- you had, in every place, you had a kapo. You know what a kapo is? A kapo is like a foreman.

INT: Yes.

SR: When we got to Gleiwitz, this particular kapo, like we had the star of David, he had a red-- a red little corner here. And he was in this concentration camp because he did not agree with-- he used to talk to me about it-- with Hitler's regime. So they put him in a concentration camp.

Nice young guy, like I would look at him now, you know? And he said that I'm going in with him. He was German. That I'm going in with him in this room, big room, where all the Germans were going in. So he took me in there.

So one-- another one told me, right away, that you're Jewish, you are Jude. You should get out of here. You don't belong here.

So mine kapo-- everybody had a-- mine kapo said to him, he should leave me alone. He told him, if you want to live through this night, you should leave this youngster alone. Otherwise, you're going to be dead in the morning. And he meant it, you know. So he stayed away from me, so I was able to sleep through the night in the inside--

INT: The inside.

SR: --that one night. You sitting on the floor, sleeping. It-- this was a wooden floor. It was not outside. And you were sitting up all night. I mean who could sleep? Yeah.

INT: How come he befriended you? Do you know why he chose you?

SR: I worked for him.

INT: Befriended you? Oh, you worked for him.

SR: I worked for him.

INT: You were the only one to work for him?

SR: No, no.

INT: Many.

SR: Many. But he couldn't take in everybody, you know what I mean?

INT: So you were fortunate?

SR: We were separated. So he kind of told me-- he called me, Simon. You know, Simon? And he was German. And he said that I'm going-- you going to walk in with me to this particular-- you're not going to stay outside, no.

I-- he-- I worked on his kommando. He had a kommando. He was our leader of the kommando. He was very nice. And he didn't do no harm. They picked him, because he was a Deutsch. He was German, you know what I mean?

And even if he had that red little corner, he did not agree, and they put him in. He told me. I just didn't agree. I spoke out, loudly. I didn't want to belong to the party. You know, and that's why he was there. Quite a few of them in this, you know?

They-- the star? The star of David was Jewish. A red little was against politics. A green was a murderer, a Verbrecher, a criminal, you know? Maybe you heard of it already if someone told you about it. Or maybe it's the first time you heard if from me. Yeah.

This was the three-- the three recognizing signs you can-- well, you had to wear it, that you could tell who they are, Jewish or you are a politician or you're a criminal.

INT: So you were in Dora, Germany, from January '45 until--

SR: I was in Dora, Germany-- oh, it took us-- January 20 was the death march. Came in the January 20. It came in 1945. It took us a day, maybe the 22nd or the 23rd. I can't think of the name. Or maybe the 24th we came into Dora.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they got us in there. We slept. They dumped us all in-- in a big-- we used to call it a kino. To us, a little-- this Dora was a little town-- a town, and the kino was the movie theater. They dumped us all in there on the floors. And again, lots of them didn't get up next morning.

Because you got up, you seeing people didn't move. They were dead. And from then, after going through the same ceremony in this camp-- a different Nazi leader, the SS, every camp had them. And they were set up this way. They-- they put us to work, OK?

And that's what I wind up in this-- in this underneath. No bomb could ever-- this place could never be bombed-- was never bombed. They made the V1, the V2, if I'm not correct.

I've seen them. I worked on them, you know, with my own hands. The wings, I got-- I put together the wings, you know? And every fellow had a guardsman watching you, what you're doing. You make a little mistake, it was no good.

INT: Was this again for private industry?

SR: [SIGHS] Well, in that particular place, you used to see civil-- they were all-- they all belong to the party, I found out at that time-- and also in uniform. The were all guarding us. Yeah, so. Must be a part-- all together, you know? All together, yeah.

INT: And how long do you stay in Dora?

SR: I think in Dora, I stayed-- I would say I stayed part of January, the whole month of February. And I think-- I think about three months, about three months. And then they-- again, the war came there already to [NON-ENGLISH], and they took us out from there, took us out of there, you know?

INT: And where did you go--

SR: And it was a rough-- Dora was a rough camp. Lots of-- like we-- I worked with a lots of Russian people, prisoners. See lots of hanging there-- very rough.

