Some fellows, they were trying to take up from the-- from that place we worked, some oil to varnish the floors, so they should be little shiny and not so dirty. And when they caught him, they hung him in the middle. Everybody had to stay straight and watch that.

One time, they hung 50-some Russians, all Russians, 50-some hung in the middle. And we had to watch it. Yeah.

INT: And what was that like for you?

SR: [SIGHS] It was very, very tough. And [INAUDIBLE] was-- my mind was made up. As long as I'm not going to get a bullet, I'm going to have to live through this. We have seen the end is coming. We have seen. We-- we have seen, because we felt it, that the end is coming. So we had to hold on. We had to hold on. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We had to hold on.

INT: And from Dora, where did you go next?

SR: From Dora, we get on trains and separated us, groups by groups. And I wind up in-- in Bergen-Belsen, Germany.

INT: And what happened then?

SR: We came in there. And they [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] came to the depot. With-- we traveled [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] we traveled in these trains. [SIGHS] I think we traveled from Dora to about-- it was about two days. Everything didn't go so smooth and took them longer. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And we got to the station, Bergen, Bergen-Belsen. Bergen is the city. Belsen was the camp. But they had a station, a small railroad station. And on that railroad station, we come. We came there.

And there were-- what you call, you make those red borscht?

INT: Beets?

SR: Beet. They had beets, piles in the station. And people were hungry. And they were trying to jump to get it. And with machine guns, brr, brr, brr, brr, like this, because we didn't get anything to eat. Like that friend what I have in Canada, Ted, Ted Milo. You see, his Jewish name is [NON-ENGLISH], you know? And he was going to run. I said to him, if you sit, get a hold of him.

And-- and they all, they falling dead on the beets, on the beets, on the beets falling dead. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

I think on that day the train was standing and standing for hours, for hours. So we were under guards. And the-- the train was here, and here was cement, like a regular train depot. But for some reason, we were able to get on that cement, on the [INAUDIBLE].

And there was a young lady walking out. I'm sure this was-- this young lady was, at that time-- where she came from? I didn't know that from Holland. You know, what's her name, that famous young lady? I spoke to her.

INT: Anne Frank?

SR: Anne Frank, with her father. Because she died in Belsen, in Bergen-Belsen. And you know, she asked me.

INT: She was on that train?

SR: She-- evidently, she was on the train. Evidently, they-- if she was on the train or she was already there, waiting, where and what, because she wind up in a conversation with me. And she asked me if I'm Yiddish. So I said, yes.

I could recall her, like I would look at her now, with her father, a tall man, you know, standing. Because they died in the-- in Bergen-Belsen. Yeah, she died, you know? I mean, whatever. And then we were taken into this camp in Belsen.

Did not do any work. This must have been already, I would say, beginning of April, putting us up in barracks. And--

INT: Did the same procedure take place? Did they disinfect you?

SR: I don't think so. I don't remember going through this. So they just-- everything was [? rushed ?] already. And like-- like already to an end. Oh, we've seen it coming. Yeah. There was no food. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

They didn't have-- in fact, the soldiers didn't have anything what to eat. They were starving, too, the German guards. They took us in, and-- and we were there till--

INT: What did you eat?

SR: I ate grass. I had-- I had-- I ate, for two weeks, grass. It was April, and it was already a little bit of sun blooming. Because that part of Germany doesn't get so cold, like-- and we pick grass, you know? And just for two weeks, two weeks. And I got the diarrhea. Almost died from it. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Yeah.

INT: Was that the only sickness you had during your thing?

SR: Well, this is about-- I was-- [SIGHS] I can tell you-- and they-- two weeks [? later, ?] and 20th of-- the 15th, 15th of-- the 15th of April, the English Army walked in.

