INT: You say the English limit-- liberated you?

SR: Yes, English army.

INT: What was liberation like?

SR: Ah, it was great. There was dancing and very happy. And I seen killings. I've seen killings, bad killings.

INT: What kind of killings?

SR: All the-- I've seen how sick I was. I is-- I was trying to get out, you know? Because the stomachs were already. And as long as the heart was beating in you, you--

I've seen killings-- the guards, the German guards, what they were there, the Nazis, they couldn't get away. In this-- in the buildings we were in, there was kind of one floor up, and they had a-- and they had an opening, like from a netting, you know?

And mostly Russian-- there were Russian prisoners. They were there liberated with us. And they took those Nazis up there, one by one. And they pushed them through, from those things. And on the bottom, when they fell on the cement, they-- they chopped them up with those picks, which you chop highways, to death; chopped them up piece by piece.

Yeah. Lots of killing. Most of them, the Russians, had done it for revenge. For revenge. Yeah.

INT: Who was with you when you were liberated?

SR: Who was with me?

INT: When you were liberated?

SR: Oh--

INT: Was your friend, Ted, with you?

SR: Friends, yeah, friends. We were a bunch of youngsters. And listen, we were all like one, like united, you know? Yeah, it was a big thing.

INT: What happened to your family? [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Do you know?

SR: To my family, what happened? I know my father was taken away, you know. And I don't know where. And my mother? My mother died in Majdanek. I know that. Yeah.

INT: How do you know that?

SR: I know that, because my sister, they were standing-- they were standing in a line. And they count people till 10. And my mother was 42, and she was 10, for some reason or another. Maybe they took her up.

