INT: And where did you go next?

SR: From Belsen-Bergen?

INT: From-- yes.

SR: Going back, so I wind up in the hospital in Celle. The city is Celle-- C-E-L-L-E. I wind up there to-- to brought me back to my health. I was nourished in a German hospital there, a Catholic hospital, by nuns. And they fed me, spoon by spoon, soup and whatever I could digest. And they brought me back to life.

INT: Did any Jewish organizations help you at that time?

SR: There was no set up. There was no-- there was no such set up in 1945. Everything was-- I mean, they didn't have it. Didn't have it. I mean, you know, they were not available. Because everything was still under-- under partial-- partial liberation. And, you know, this was the 15th of-- of April.

INT: And after the hospital?

SR: [SIGHS] After the hospital, I think it took me about either two or three months-- I recovered. Because by the liberation I only weighed about 78 pounds, so.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

