INT: What did you do then?

SR: Well, after the liberation, we didn't do much. We were-- when I got to mine power again, I could walk and move around. We started to look for families, you know? And this was our main purpose, if we can find a family-- a mother, father, cousin, a sister, you know.

Because everybody was taken apart. And we didn't know if they're alive or not. Didn't have hopes for my parents, as I found out. But I had hope for my sister, you know?

INT: How did you go about looking for them?

SR: I-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] in the camp, I met Cyla, my wife, which is my wife now. And she had a mother. And first I did-- what I did is I put up signs in every corner.

Belsen-Bergen was a big, big camp. And there were-- they had a committee there already set up-- offices. People look for each other. Right away, they were set up. It was not computerized, but by mouth, by advertising, whatever they could.

And I put up signs that my name is so-and-so, Shulim Rafowicz, and I'm looking for my sister, Helen. Anybody know should come to this barrack, room number so-and-so, and tell me about it.

So I was-- on that particular day, I was traveling to a city, Osnabrück, Germany. I was there with-- [POINTS] she was not-- she was-- we just met.

I went there. The mayor from this city was with me in the camp. He became mayor of the city.

INT: He was mayor before the war or after the war?

SR: This was-- this was after the liberation.

INT: After liberation.

SR: You asked me what I did.

INT: He became mayor of that city?

SR: He was also a prisoner for war. And he said, if the war will end, he will take over the city of Osnabrück.

INT: OK.

SR: Because he was also against it, [INAUDIBLE]. And he told me-- we got to know each other. We talked. He told me-- he told me, Simon-- he calls me Simon-- if you need something, you come into this city. And I will be mayor there. Because we talked already, like. The war was already to an end.

So you know, when you're liberated, you didn't have any clothing, you know. Like mine-- my wife had a gray coat with the Star of David-- am I correct? With a patch in the back. This--

CYLA BONK: Front and back.

SR: Front and back, Star of David. So I said to her, why don't you go with me to the city, Osnabrück? I have-- as I said, mayor from the city, we probably get some clothing for you.

So her mother did not allow her to take her on a train, you know, with me there. So I promised her that she's going to stay in a different place with a different German family. Don't worry. I'm going to set it up. And she's going to be in good hands.

And second, I said, your daughter's 19-- 18, 19. You can trust her, you know? Well, at that, you know, everything-- parents were afraid. She didn't know me, and I can't blame her. So finally, she allowed to go the train.

To make a story short, we seen that man. He took us to a department store. And he gave orders to fix her up, whatever she wants.

On the way back, when the train stopped, some friends saw me. And they said, Sam, we have good news. Your sister's alive. OK. So, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] so this was good news to me at that time. And--

INT: Where was she?

SR: They told me that she's in the city in Austria, Judenburg-- J-U-D-E-N-B-R-G. So I came back that evening into Belsen-Bergen. And I told mine Cyla that I'm taking off for Austria [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] to look for my sister-- which I did.

INT: How did you travel?

SR: By train. I had-- after liberation, after you got back, they gave you a little card. And on the card, it says your name. And it says, DP-- Displaced Person. And they honored this little card on all the trains. You didn't have to buy a ticket. You can travel all Germany with this ticket.

So I took off. I took off by train-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] by train, but I couldn't get into Austria. So I traveled into the border of Germany and Austria. I came in there to a place, which it was a hotel.

And I got a hold of the lady. She-- she owned-- the German woman, she owned this hotel. And I told her what my purpose is. She told me, I don't mind you rest up here. But this hotel is occupied by American soldiers. No civils can stay overnight here. So she said to me--

And I had a brief-- a kind of small, little briefcase of cigarettes. Her mother gave it to me. I didn't smoke. But she said, with cigarettes-- at that time was a shortage, and people were smoking. You can, maybe with a package or so, you can get places, you know?

So I had about a couple cartons, you know. The reason why they gave us cigarettes in the camps, you know. At that time, nobody knew about cigarettes. Everybody smoked, I mean the soldiers and them.

So when I got to this-- when I told the owner from this particular hotel what my purpose is-- I'm on the border-- she told me, I'm going to get you a young man. And he's going to direct you. He's right here.

I said, what do I do? I had no money, you know. And I said-- I said I have cigarettes. So, OK, give him some cigarette. The young men came, and I gave him those cigarettes. He never showed up.

I said to the lady, I said, lady, you live on the border here. You live on the border here. Why don't you tell me how to cross the border? I never tell anybody that you told me. I got to get there. I got to cross the border.

Well, she have seen what I'm after. And she took me. And she said, go this patch. Go this patch and walk. And you're going cross the border.

So I did it at night. And I walked, and I walked in the middle of the night. And I walked through a-- through a little pass, you know, what you see at night till I saw a light. And I was very tired. And I said, whatever it's going to happen, I have to stop here.

So I stopped, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] knocked, knocked at this door. A man came to the-- to the-- to the door. I saw on a light in the window. And he, when he opened his mouth, he had that Austrian accent. I have seen that I am in Austria.

And I told him my purpose. He took me in. It was about 4 or 5 o'clock in the morning. And the wife woke up. They gave me some milk. They want me to lay down, which I didn't want. I was scared. And-- and they told me, when it's going to get more light in the morning, they going to show me the direct to go in to-- the way to go into Salzburg, Austria.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And meanwhile-- and this was so, they showed me the way. And I-- walking into Salzburg was a long way. Another fellow hooked up with me, which he was not Jewish. I spoke to him. He was a prisoner of war. He got wind up there walking, you know?

So before we got into the city, police got a hold of us. And I showed them-- I talked to them, and I told them my purpose. I got to go into Judenburg. I have a sister, and I'm a displaced person, blah, blah.

And they heard of it. They didn't heard of it. But the police let me go. They took the other guy. They let me go.

So whether-- finally, I made it to Judenburg. When I came into Judenburg, my sister was not there anymore. They told me they took off for Italy. OK?

So I said, I am there. I got to go to Italy. Where to Italy, I don't know. Italy is a good country-- is a big country.

And so I-- I went to the train again in there. And I traveled into-- I think, from Judenburg, I went back into Salzburg, because Salzburg was a big city. Judenburg was-- I found out there was-- they were there. It was kind of a kibbutz, a place, a station over for them.

And I'm standing there on the depot to cross to go into Italy. I can't walk to Italy. So I got a whole for-- I got a hold of that-- of a person, who is in charge of the trains, Austrian, you know? And I spoke to him. And I told him, I have some cigarettes I'd be glad to give it to you.

So he told me, about midnight, a train is coming in with Italian prisoners coming back from Russia. You get into this train with them. And about midnight or 1 o'clock at night, that train got in. The train stopped. And he made sure that I got in, in the train.

And I was blond. I had a gray-- a-- a blue air force jacket on. That's what they gave me in camp. I looked like a German. The-- when I got into the train, the Italians jumped at me, and they screamed. They screamed at me, [ITALIAN], [ITALIAN]. [ITALIAN] means, 'You German,' 'You German.'

I didn't know what [ITALIAN] means. I didn't know why they screaming at me. So finally, one jumped up, and he asked me if I speak German. And I said, yes. He said, and who are you? I told them.

And quiet down those guys. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Still had a few cigarettes, I-- they were hungry. And, and I started-- said, help yourself. And we crossed the border.

[PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

