He said, but on the-- when this train's going to stop in Trento, on the station, he said, we going to-- we going to get a priest up here. You sit with us, and the priest going to come up. He's going to bless us. He knew all about it.

So you-- you just-- he's going to bless you. You just sit and say, grazie. And he put some water over me, you know? And I said, grazie. And the train took off. And Trento. And I opened the door. It's just a small, short [INAUDIBLE]. And I jumped the train. So I was in Italy already.

And I walked and walked. It took me about two or three days there. I didn't know where I'm going.

And finally, I wind up-- I wind up to a place. I went from police, in the cities there, in Italy, and to Jewish families that were there, which they could not speak any other language but just Italian-- Italian. And we couldn't understand-- I could recognize that they were Jewish people. They couldn't help me.

And I went in to-- they were-- had some camps with Poles-- Polish people. They couldn't help me. So I don't know. I wind up, and they told me that Reggio Emilia is a big, big camp there.

And, and that's where I found my sister, at Reggio Emilia, in a kibbutz, you know? A big story.

INT: Did she know you were alive?

SR: No. No. They told her that I was trying to escape, and they killed me.

INT: Oh, my--

SR: So, on this-- walking this highway in Italy, in 1945, November, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] an older man-- how did I get to this camp? An older man, in Reggio Emilia, told me [ITALIAN]-- [ITALIAN] means Jewish people-- I should walk avanti. 'Avanti' means, walk straight.

